You can't — the Vite maintainers declined to accept a function for `resolve.conditions` "for now as it's difficult to merge if other plugins were to add more conditions" [4]. Instead, import Vite's default list and spread it yourself: use `defaultClientConditions` for client builds or `defaultServerConditions` for server builds [4]. For example:

```js
import { defaultClientConditions } from 'vite'

export default defineConfig({
  resolve: {
    conditions: ['workspace', ...defaultClientConditions],
  },
})
```

`defaultClientConditions` is documented at https://vite.dev/config/shared-options#resolve-conditions [4], and a utility like the one linked in the thread may also help [4].

The failure is an upstream Rolldown bug, likely caused by rolldown/rolldown#9964, which affects the `lazyBarrel` feature [2]. You can work around it by disabling that feature in your config [2][3][4]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  build: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      experimental: {
        lazyBarrel: false,
      },
    },
  },
}
```

Alternatively, upgrading to Rolldown v1.1.4 should work, since rolldown/rolldown#10071 temporarily disabled `lazyBarrel` [1].

In Vite 8.0.13, const enums used as types in casting disappear from the bundled output, whereas in 8.0.12 they were still present as regular compiled enums [4]. The casting causes the enum to not be included in the outputted code [3]. This is a regression tracked as issue #22452 [1][4].

To work around it, avoid the direct `as` part [3]:
```ts
class Foo {
  // casting causes Phase to not be in the outputted code
  #foo = ([Phase.idle] as [Phase])[0];

  test() {
    return this.#foo;
  }
}
```

The fix is available in 8.0.14 [1].

The failure is a Rolldown CJS namespace interop issue, not something specific to `backbone-relational`: a CJS namespace import snapshots the current `module.exports` properties before a later CJS side-effect import mutates the same export object [2]. That's why `import * as Backbone from 'backbone'` followed by `import 'backbone-relational'` works in Vite dev but fails in the Vite 8 build [2]. Default and named imports still work because they keep/read the original CJS export object [2]. The maintainers consider the durable fix to be in Rolldown first, with Vite adding regression coverage afterward [2].

Workaround — use a default import instead of a namespace import [1]:
```js
import Backbone from "backbone";
import "backbone-relational";

Backbone.RelationalModel.extend({});
```

Your `@layer` ordering statement is being dropped during production minification when the ordering statement and the `@layer {}` blocks live in different imported CSS files. With the default `minify: 'esbuild'`, the bare `@layer a, b, c;` statement is removed, so layer precedence falls back to block appearance order (import/concat order) and the cascade silently changes [1][5]. This only happens with multiple files plus minification: `minify: false` preserves the statement, and single-file CSS gets its blocks reordered to match the statement [1]. In the reproduction, `base` is declared lowest but ends up highest, overriding what it should lose to [5].

```css
/* order.css */
@layer base, reset, utilities;
@layer base { .c { color: green } }
/* vendor.css */
@layer reset { .a { color: red } }
@layer utilities { .b { color: blue } }
```

A related cause is file order itself: even when the ordering statement is preserved, the resulting order can differ because of the order in which files are concatenated [2]. Note that the first occurrence of a `@layer` name establishes its cascade position, so a declaration appearing before an explicit ordering takes precedence [3].

There's no built-in Vite option for this yet; the `maxWarnings` config option is only a suggested solution in the open issue [1]. A maintainer prefers keeping it in a plugin for now [2]. You can implement it with a plugin that counts warnings via `onLog` and throws in `buildEnd` [3][4]:

```ts
function failOnWarningsPlugin(): Rolldown.Plugin {
  let warningCount = 0;
  return {
    name: 'fail-on-warnings',
    onLog(level) {
      if (level === 'warn') warningCount++;
    },
    buildEnd() {
      if (!warningCount) return;
      throw new Error(`${warningCount} warnings present`);
    },
  };
}
```

Note that throwing will also log an error stack trace, and `process.exit` is discouraged due to Node.js behaviors like not flushing stdio buffers [5].

The failure is a compatibility issue with esbuild version resolution after upgrading Vite: legacy builds start failing once `esbuild@0.27.7` is in use, even with a basic `legacy({ targets: ['defaults', 'not IE 11'] })` config [2][3]. Forcing `esbuild` back to `0.27.4` via an override makes the problem disappear, confirming it's tied to the newer esbuild version rather than the plugin config [3]. The build fails during the legacy downgrade/transformation step [3]. It was fixed by #22346 in Vite 7.3.3 [4], and the underlying cause is tracked upstream in evanw/esbuild#4436 [5].

```json
{
  "overrides": {
    "esbuild": "0.27.4"
  }
}
```

Vite 8's dependency optimization reorders ECharts internals: in the optimized output, the `install` method runs before `init_install` has executed, causing `Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'type')`, whereas Vite 5.4.11 ran `init_install` first [4]. The issue is labeled "bug: upstream" and is reported to work with Vite 8.0.14+ [1]. As a workaround for dev, set `optimizeDeps.rolldownOptions.output.strictExecutionOrder: true` [2]:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  optimizeDeps: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      output: {
        strictExecutionOrder: true
      }
    }
  },
});
```

The excerpts do not explain the underlying cause beyond the upstream label and a possible relation to issue #21866 [3].

Vite's library mode is intended for browser-oriented libraries, so `import.meta.url` isn't rewritten to a Node-style value by default [1]. The `platform` default you're relying on (`'node'` for `'cjs'`) is Rolldown's default, not Vite's — and `platform` didn't exist in Vite v7 because Rollup has no such option [3]. As a result, the emitted code becomes `fileURLToPath({}.url)`, which throws `ERR_INVALID_ARG_TYPE` [2]. The fix is to set the platform explicitly:

```js
build: {
  rolldownOptions: {
    platform: 'node'
  }
}
```

With that, the output uses `require("url").pathToFileURL(__filename).href` instead [2].

It's a TypeScript limitation, not a Vite bug: the async config's returned object literal widens `minify` to `string`, which doesn't satisfy the expected union type, producing error 2769 [3]. The fix is to add `as const` [2]:

```ts
export default defineConfig(async () => ({
  plugins: [react()],
  build: {
    minify: 'terser',
  },
} as const))
```

Vite 7.2.x strips `/*! */` comments because they're treated as "legal comments" by most tools, and esbuild's minification removes legal comments by default [1]. As bluwy explains, `/*! */` "isn't a way to mark 'important'" — that's a PurgeCSS convention, not a robust signal, so relying on it breaks when legal comments are stripped [1]. The suggested fix is to either run PurgeCSS before esbuild minifies (e.g., via postcss) or configure esbuild to keep them [1][4]. The confirmed workaround is setting `esbuild.legalComments: 'inline'` [2][4]:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  esbuild: {
    legalComments: 'inline',
  },
})
```

Yes. Starting in 7.1.8, vite-rolldown removed comments even with `minify: false`, including `/* @vite-ignore */` and `/* webpackIgnore: true */` annotations [4]. This was fixed in Vite 8.0.10 [2]. As a workaround, put the comment before the string rather than on the variable reference [3]:

```js
export async function init() {
  const bar = /* @vite-ignore */ /* webpackIgnore: true */ 'some-url'.slice(0);
  return await import(bar);
}
```

The thread attributes this to module execution order: when lazy loading is involved, the code that defines the global (e.g. the `Zo` function defining `Prism`) can run after the code that accesses it (`Prism.languages.clike`), producing `Uncaught ReferenceError: Prism is not defined` [4]. The reporter notes it only happens with lazy loading, when the importing file gets its own JS file at build time, and when the call order changes between working and broken code [4]. A maintainer says it now works without `strictExecutionOrder` since Vite 8.0.6+ [3], though another user still hit the error on 8.0.6 [1]. As a workaround, enabling `strictExecutionOrder` in `rolldownOptions.output` worked for one user [5]:

```js
build: {
  rolldownOptions: {
    output: {
      strictExecutionOrder: true,
    },
  },
}
```

The excerpts do not identify the underlying root cause beyond this ordering behavior.

You can't currently get both behaviors with `server.proxy`'s `auth` option: when the client sends its own `authorization` header, the `auth`-generated `Authorization: Basic ...` header is not sent to the upstream [1]. This is described as a bug in http-proxy (issue #1706), which is bundled within Vite [1].

The configuration that triggers this is:

```js
const proxy = {
  '/api/service1': {
    target: 'http://localhost:80/endpoint/requiring/basic-auth',
    auth: 'user1:pass1',
  },
};
```

With that config, a request like `fetch('/api/service1', { headers: { authorization: "anything" } })` causes the upstream basic auth header to be dropped [1]. The excerpts don't provide a workaround or a fix for passing both headers simultaneously.

The hang is an upstream Rollup issue: Rollup changed the default of `maxParallelFileOps`, which caused `--watch` to get stuck on rebuild in Vite v6 [3]. It was fixed in rollup v4.44.0 [1]. As a workaround, set `build.rollupOptions.maxParallelFileOps: 100` [2][4]:

```js
export default {
  build: {
    rollupOptions: {
      maxParallelFileOps: 100,
    },
  },
}
```

If that alone gives a `[commonjs] Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'resolved')` error, adding `build.commonjsOptions.strictRequires: 'auto'` reportedly resolves it [5].

Because Sass's modern API mangles the path before Vite sees it: Sass passes `@/../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/_functions` to Vite as `node_modules/bootstrap/scss/_functions`, so the alias segment is lost [2]. This is a limitation of Sass's modern API, which is the default from Vite 6, and there isn't an easy way to solve it [2][3]. The issue was closed as won't-fix for that reason [1].

As a workaround, set the legacy API:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  css: {
    preprocessorOptions: {
      sass: { api: 'legacy' } // or scss: { api: 'legacy' }
    }
  }
}
```

One user confirmed this fixed the problem using `css.preprocessorOptions.scss.api: 'legacy'` [4].

You can't fully turn off the WebSocket: a Vite maintainer states "WebSocket is used for features other than HMR so it cannot be disabled" [4]. Setting `server.hmr: false` disables HMR itself, but the WebSocket connection still exists, which is why you may still see "failed to connect to WebSocket" errors [1][4].

There's also a known bug where a standalone WebSocket server is still created on port 24678 even when `hmr: false` (e.g. in middleware/SSR mode), because `createWebSocketServer` falls into the `else` branch when `wsServer` is falsy [3].

As a temporary workaround, one user patched `setupWebSocket` to return early and ignore the socket [5]:

```js
function setupWebSocket(protocol, hostAndPath, onCloseWithoutOpen) {
    return null; // return and ignore websocket
    const socket = new WebSocket(`${protocol}://${hostAndPath}`,'vite-hmr');
    let isOpened = false;
```

Note that these are pending-triage issues, so no official option to disable the WebSocket is documented in the excerpts.
